        <!-- CONTENT -->
        <div class="content">

            <p>Step on a scale and you see one number: your weight. But that number hides the most important detail — <strong>what that weight is actually made of.</strong> Muscle? Fat? Water? Bone?</p>
            <p>That's where <strong>body fat percentage</strong> comes in. It's the single most useful number for understanding your real fitness level — even more useful than <a href="/blog/what-is-bmi/">BMI</a>. Let's break it down in simple words.</p>

            <h2 id="what-is-it">What Is Body Fat Percentage?</h2>
            <p>Body fat percentage is simply <strong>how much of your total weight is fat</strong>, expressed as a percentage.</p>

            <div class="formula-box">
                <div class="f-label">The Simple Formula</div>
                <div class="f-main">Body Fat % = (Fat Mass ÷ Total Weight) × 100</div>
                <div class="f-note">Everything else — muscle, bone, water, organs — is called "lean mass."</div>
            </div>

            <div class="example-box">
                <div class="e-title">⚖️ Quick Example</div>
                <div class="step"><b>Your weight:</b> 80 kg</div>
                <div class="step"><b>Your fat mass:</b> 20 kg</div>
                <div class="result">✅ Body Fat % = (20 ÷ 80) × 100 = 25%</div>
            </div>

            <p>Here's why this matters: two people can weigh exactly the same and have the same BMI — but completely different bodies. As we explained in <a href="/blog/is-bmi-accurate/">Is BMI Accurate?</a>, BMI can't tell muscle from fat. Body fat percentage can.</p>

            <h2 id="healthy-ranges">Healthy Body Fat Ranges (Chart)</h2>
            <p>What's "healthy" depends on your <strong>gender</strong> — women naturally need more essential fat for hormonal and reproductive health. Here are the standard ranges from the American Council on Exercise (ACE):</p>

            <div class="table-wrap">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Category</th>
                            <th>Men</th>
                            <th>Women</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Essential fat</strong> (minimum to survive)</td>
                            <td>2–5%</td>
                            <td>10–13%</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Athletes</strong></td>
                            <td>6–13%</td>
                            <td>14–20%</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Fitness</strong> 💪</td>
                            <td>14–17%</td>
                            <td>21–24%</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Average</strong> ✅</td>
                            <td>18–24%</td>
                            <td>25–31%</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Obese</strong> ⚠️</td>
                            <td>25%+</td>
                            <td>32%+</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <p><strong>Key takeaway:</strong> For most men, <strong>10–20%</strong> is a healthy target. For most women, <strong>18–28%</strong> is healthy. And remember — lower is <em>not</em> always better. Dropping below essential fat levels is dangerous, not impressive.</p>

            <h2 id="how-to-measure">How to Measure Body Fat</h2>
            <p>There are several ways to measure body fat — from free home methods to expensive lab scans:</p>

            <div class="table-wrap">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Method</th>
                            <th>Cost</th>
                            <th>Accuracy</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>US Navy method</strong> (tape measure)</td>
                            <td>Free 🎉</td>
                            <td>Good estimate (±3–4%)</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Smart scales</strong> (bioimpedance)</td>
                            <td>₹₹</td>
                            <td>Varies with hydration</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Skinfold calipers</strong></td>
                            <td>₹</td>
                            <td>Good if done correctly</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>DEXA scan</strong></td>
                            <td>₹₹₹₹</td>
                            <td>Gold standard 🥇</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <p>The easiest way to start? The <strong>US Navy method</strong> — you only need a measuring tape and 30 seconds. Our <a href="/body-fat-calculator/">free body fat calculator</a> uses exactly this method. No equipment, no cost.</p>

            <div class="calc-cta">
                <h3>What's Your Body Fat %? 📊</h3>
                <p>Find out in 30 seconds with the US Navy method — just a measuring tape needed.</p>
                <a href="/body-fat-calculator/" class="btn-white">Calculate My Body Fat →</a>
            </div>

            <h2 id="how-to-lower">How to Lower Body Fat Safely</h2>
            <p>If your number is higher than you'd like, here's the honest, science-backed approach:</p>

            <ol>
                <li><strong>Create a moderate calorie deficit</strong> — eat slightly fewer calories than you burn. Not sure how many? Check our guide on <a href="/blog/how-many-calories-should-i-eat-a-day/">how many calories you should eat a day</a>.</li>
                <li><strong>Prioritize protein</strong> — protein protects your muscle while you lose fat. Use our <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> to find your daily target.</li>
                <li><strong>Strength train 2–4 times a week</strong> — muscle is metabolically active tissue. More muscle = higher metabolism = easier fat loss.</li>
                <li><strong>Be patient</strong> — a safe rate is losing <strong>0.5–1% body fat per month</strong>. Faster usually means you're losing muscle too.</li>
                <li><strong>Sleep 7–9 hours</strong> — poor sleep raises hunger hormones and makes fat loss significantly harder.</li>
            </ol>

            <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
            <p>Your weight is just a number. Your <strong>body fat percentage</strong> is the real story. Track it monthly, combine it with your <a href="/bmi-calculator/">BMI</a> and waist measurement, and focus on trends — not daily fluctuations. Progress beats perfection. 💚</p>

            <div class="note">
                <strong>⚠️ A quick note:</strong> Home body fat measurements are estimates, not lab results. Use them to track trends over time, and consult a healthcare professional for personalized advice.
            </div>

